course: dictionary
lesson: 1878
title: Collateral: a word with two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Some words belong to special areas of life, and collateral is one of them. In money talk, collateral is something valuable that you promise to give a bank if you cannot pay back a loan, such as a house. Grown-ups say you «put up» something as collateral, and the bank keeps it as a safety until the money is paid back.
explain: The second meaning is about harm. «Collateral damage» means harm done by accident to people or things that were not the target, especially in war. Official, formal writing uses it so that harm sounds like a neat number in a report. In everyday talk, people sometimes use it half-jokingly for any side harm.
explain: To work out which meaning you are reading, look at the words around it. If you see loan, bank or promise, it is about money. If you see damage, attack or war, it is about harm that was not meant. Then swap your meaning into the sentence to check it still makes sense.
fact: Collateral means something valuable that you promise to give a bank if you cannot pay back a loan.
fact: «Put up as collateral» means offered as a safety for a loan.
fact: «Collateral damage» means harm done by accident to people or things that were not the target.
fact: «Collateral damage» is official military language, so you will often find it in news and formal reports.
fact: In everyday talk, «collateral damage» can be used half-jokingly for any harm that happens as a side effect.
exercise: The bank asked for collateral before it lent the money. What is collateral here? | A kind of loud alarm | A gift given to the bank | *Something valuable promised to the bank in case the loan is not paid back | A type of bank card
exercise: «The farmer put up his land as collateral.» What did he do with his land? | He planted trees on it | He sold it to a neighbour for a picnic | *He promised it to the bank in case he could not repay the loan | He painted it red
exercise: In the phrase «collateral damage», what does collateral mean? | *Harm done by accident to people or things that were not the target | A loud noise after a storm | A large, valuable prize | A loan from a friend
test: Why do official reports sometimes use cold words like «collateral damage»? | Because the words mean exactly the same as a hug | To make the report shorter and funnier | To show that everyone in the report is happy | *So that harm sounds like a number in a report instead of hurting real people
test: Ben says his soggy picnic and wrecked ball game were «collateral damage» from the storm, half-jokingly. What does he mean? | *The storm hurt those things by accident, as a side effect | He borrowed money from the storm | The storm was the target he wanted to hit | He is talking about a real war
exam: Which is the best meaning of collateral damage? | A prize for winning a race | A valuable thing you promise to a bank | A loud alarm that wakes everyone | *Harm done by accident to people or things that were not the target
